A||||5||FieroPerformance||V6 clutch with an Isuzu 5 speed? HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||10-20-2001||08:53 PM||||K, I've got an Isuzu 5 speed hooked up to my 2.8. I have a brand new clutch, pressure plate and t/o bearing for a V6. The clutch and pressure plate bolt onto the flywheel fine and everything would work great if the throwout bearing would fit onto the tranny, but it doesn't. The t/o bearing that was on the tranny looks like it is just a hair too small to work the v6 pressure plate correctly. I went to AutoZone and got a new throw out bearing for an Isuzu 5 speed and it is a slightly different designd. From looking at it I think it would work but I wanted to check the forum and see if anyone has ever tried this and what their results were?<P>Thanks a lot. I can post pics of everything if needed.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||pherder||10-20-2001||08:57 PM||||You need to use the throwout bearing that fits the transaxle.  <P>I had an Isuzu 5 speed and the dual friction clutch/pressure plate from Centerforce in my old Quad four powered 88 Coupe and ask Standard how it is working!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroPerformance||10-20-2001||11:03 PM||||Alright thanks. But, has anyone ever used a V6 clutch and pressure plate with an Isuzu throwout bearing and tranny?<P>-Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||10-20-2001||11:08 PM|||| You might also find that the clutch will bind up in the transaxle bellhousing webbing. The V6 clutch is bigger in diameter than the 5 spd isuzu clutch.<P> You will be better off getting the proper clutch for that transaxle.<P> You can take an alternate route if you insist on using the V6 clutch.<P>I used a dual friction V6 clutch with the isuzu release bearing in the isuzu tranny on my V8. What I had to do was grind soem of the webbing and release bearing shaft boss away to clear the clutch.<P> Heres a poor quality pic, but the shiny spots is where i removed material<P>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/grind%20isuzu%20to%20clear%204spd%20clutch!.BMP">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||10-20-2001||11:16 PM||||Great, I've got a few other little questions.<P>1. What was the part number of the throwout bearing that you used?<P>2. Have you had any problems.<P>I am sending you an email just in case you don't check this post again.<P>Thanks!<BR>-Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000005||jelly2m8||10-20-2001||11:30 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroPerformance:<BR><B>Great, I've got a few other little questions.<P>1. What was the part number of the throwout bearing that you used?<P>2. Have you had any problems.<P>I am sending you an email just in case you don't check this post again.<P>Thanks!<BR>-Charlie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> I used a centerforce release bearing for a '85 Fiero, 2.5l 5spd ( isuzu). and a dual friction clutch for a '85 V6 4spd.<P> I think the box is still kicking around in the shop, I'll get you the number if its still there.<P> Havent had any troubles after I had enough material removed. I crank on that V8 pretty heavy sometimes, and nothing has gone BOOM.<P> I found this out the hard way years ago when a customer wanted a 4 spd in his 86 4 cyl fiero. I used his clutch from the 5 spd.<P> Everything went together fine, but the engine would make a partial roll and bind up.<BR> I took it back apart and used a 5 spd clutch in it.<P> Moving on to the V8, I figgured as much would happen again, so what i did was took an old isuzu clutch, bolted it and the tranny up on the cradle. From there i used a strong arm on the crank pulley bolt and forced the engine over several times. <P> I took the transaxle back off, and ground some material away where I seen there was an interferance issue. Then bolted it all back together again, and repeated the process.<P> It took 3 tries to get enough material out, not much BTW, to clear the V6 clutch. Then I took just a shade more out of it so hopefully there will be enough clearance in the future in case there are minor differences in the pressure plate.<P> Hope this helps!!<p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
